<h2>The problem in one ordersheet</h2>
<p>The husband&rsquo;s restitution petition was filed in 2025. Notices went out; none were served. The wife never appeared, never engaged, never undertook anything. Yet on 18 March 2026 the Family Court issued bailable warrants &mdash; the kind of process ordinarily reserved for a party who has been duly summoned and has chosen absence. The court cited Supreme Court authority for using coercive measures in matrimonial cases. The difficulty was that the authority says no such thing about an unserved respondent.</p>

<h2>What Jagraj Singh actually decided</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <strong>The precedent&rsquo;s facts</strong>
    <p>In <em>Jagraj Singh v. Birpal Kaur</em> (2007) 2 SCC 564, the respondent wife knew of the proceeding, had given assurances to the court and had participated in reconciliation efforts. Coercive process against her enforced engagement she had already begun.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <strong>This case</strong>
    <p>Summons never served; no appearance; no assurances; no reconciliation stage reached. Warrants were the first thing the respondent would ever have heard from the court — compulsion without notice.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The High Court&rsquo;s conclusion followed: the order &ldquo;may not be termed as one passed in accordance with law.&rdquo; Precedent travels with its facts, and a ruling about a participating litigant cannot justify warrants against an unserved one.</p>

<h2>The procedural baseline for matrimonial cases</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Serve first.</strong> Personal service, substituted service where genuinely necessary — but some lawful mode must be completed before any adverse or coercive step.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Then attempt settlement.</strong> Order XXXIIA Rule 3 CPC and Section 23(2) of the Hindu Marriage Act oblige the court to attempt reconciliation — which presupposes a respondent who knows there is something to be reconciled.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Escalate only on defiance.</strong> Coercive process answers disobedience by a party with knowledge, not silence from a party without it.</div>
</div>

<h2>A note on the statutory setting</h2>
<p>The proceeding was framed under Section 21 of Uttarakhand&rsquo;s Uniform Civil Code, 2024 &mdash; among the early reported instances of conjugal-rights litigation under that state code reaching a High Court. The procedural discipline, though, is universal: in Delhi&rsquo;s Family Courts under the Hindu Marriage Act, as in Rudrapur under the UCC, jurisdiction over a respondent begins with service, and every coercive power downstream depends on it.</p>
<div class="note">This report summarises a judgment of the Uttarakhand High Court dated 25 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice.</div>
